{"schemaVersion":"1.0.0","id":"builtin-curated-release-001-group-001","title":"Astronomy and Space: First Light / Computation Challenge: Mental Gymnastics","description":"Two curated 15-question sets.","version":"1.0.0","language":"en-US","contentType":"curated-sets","categories":["Astronomy and Space","Computation"],"questions":[{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q01","level":1,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["planets"],"prompt":"Which planet is closest to the Sun?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Earth"},{"id":"b","text":"Mars"},{"id":"c","text":"Venus"},{"id":"d","text":"Mercury"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"It has the shortest year in the Solar System.","explanation":"Mercury is the innermost planet.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q02","level":2,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["planets","Saturn"],"prompt":"Which planet is famous for its prominent ring system?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Mercury"},{"id":"b","text":"Venus"},{"id":"c","text":"Mars"},{"id":"d","text":"Saturn"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Its rings are made largely of ice particles.","explanation":"Saturn has the Solar System's most visually prominent rings.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q03","level":3,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["galaxies"],"prompt":"Which is the nearest large galaxy to the Milky Way?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"The Sombrero Galaxy"},{"id":"b","text":"The Triangulum Galaxy"},{"id":"c","text":"The Andromeda Galaxy"},{"id":"d","text":"The Whirlpool Galaxy"}],"correctChoiceId":"c","hint":"It is also catalogued as M31.","explanation":"Andromeda is the nearest major galaxy to the Milky Way.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q04","level":4,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["eclipses"],"prompt":"During a total solar eclipse, which body passes between Earth and the Sun?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Venus"},{"id":"b","text":"The Moon"},{"id":"c","text":"Mars"},{"id":"d","text":"Mercury"}],"correctChoiceId":"b","hint":"Its apparent size can cover the solar disk.","explanation":"A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon blocks the Sun from Earth's viewpoint.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q05","level":5,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["planets","Venus"],"prompt":"Which planet is usually the brightest planet in Earth's night sky?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Venus"},{"id":"b","text":"Mars"},{"id":"c","text":"Jupiter"},{"id":"d","text":"Saturn"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"It is often called the morning or evening star.","explanation":"Venus appears exceptionally bright because it is nearby and highly reflective.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q06","level":6,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["stars"],"prompt":"What is a supernova?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"A newborn planet"},{"id":"b","text":"A powerful stellar explosion"},{"id":"c","text":"A small asteroid"},{"id":"d","text":"A lunar crater"}],"correctChoiceId":"b","hint":"It can mark the death of a massive star or white dwarf.","explanation":"A supernova is an extremely energetic stellar explosion.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q07","level":7,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["Solar System","Kuiper Belt"],"prompt":"The Kuiper Belt lies mainly beyond the orbit of which planet?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Jupiter"},{"id":"b","text":"Mars"},{"id":"c","text":"Saturn"},{"id":"d","text":"Neptune"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Pluto is one of its best-known members.","explanation":"The Kuiper Belt is a region of icy bodies beyond Neptune.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q08","level":8,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["cosmology"],"prompt":"The cosmic microwave background is relic radiation from what event?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"The formation of the Moon"},{"id":"b","text":"The first solar eclipse"},{"id":"c","text":"The early hot universe after the Big Bang"},{"id":"d","text":"A nearby supernova"}],"correctChoiceId":"c","hint":"It fills space almost uniformly.","explanation":"The cosmic microwave background is cooled radiation from the early universe.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q09","level":9,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["spectroscopy","cosmology"],"prompt":"Astronomical redshift means observed wavelengths are shifted in which direction?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Toward shorter wavelengths"},{"id":"b","text":"Randomly with no pattern"},{"id":"c","text":"Only toward ultraviolet"},{"id":"d","text":"Toward longer wavelengths"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Red light has longer wavelengths than blue light.","explanation":"Redshift is a displacement toward longer wavelengths.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q10","level":10,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["orbital mechanics"],"prompt":"What is the Roche limit?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"The edge of the observable universe"},{"id":"b","text":"The maximum brightness of a star"},{"id":"c","text":"The distance within which tidal forces can disrupt a satellite"},{"id":"d","text":"The minimum speed for orbit"}],"correctChoiceId":"c","hint":"It helps explain why rings can form.","explanation":"Inside the Roche limit, tidal forces may overcome a body's self-gravity.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q11","level":11,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["distance ladder","stars"],"prompt":"Cepheid variable stars are especially useful for measuring what?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Planetary magnetic fields"},{"id":"b","text":"Solar wind speed"},{"id":"c","text":"Meteorite ages only"},{"id":"d","text":"Astronomical distances"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Their period is related to their luminosity.","explanation":"Cepheids are standard candles because their pulsation period reveals intrinsic brightness.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q12","level":12,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["black holes","relativity"],"prompt":"The Schwarzschild radius corresponds to what for a non-rotating black hole?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Its accretion disk's outer edge"},{"id":"b","text":"Its magnetic pole"},{"id":"c","text":"Its maximum temperature"},{"id":"d","text":"Its event-horizon radius"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Crossing this radius prevents escape.","explanation":"For a non-rotating black hole, the Schwarzschild radius defines the event horizon.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q13","level":13,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["orbital mechanics"],"prompt":"What is a barycenter?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"The common center of mass around which bodies orbit"},{"id":"b","text":"A galaxy's visible edge"},{"id":"c","text":"A telescope mirror defect"},{"id":"d","text":"A star's hottest layer"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Earth and the Moon both orbit one.","explanation":"A barycenter is the center of mass of an orbiting system.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q14","level":14,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["stars","chemistry"],"prompt":"In stellar astronomy, metallicity refers to the abundance of what?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Elements heavier than helium"},{"id":"b","text":"Only iron"},{"id":"c","text":"Hydrogen and helium exclusively"},{"id":"d","text":"Only radioactive elements"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Astronomers use 'metals' more broadly than chemists.","explanation":"Stellar metallicity measures the fraction of elements heavier than helium.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01-q15","level":15,"category":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["astrophysics","accretion"],"prompt":"The Eddington luminosity is reached when radiation pressure balances what?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"Inward gravity"},{"id":"b","text":"Dark-energy expansion"},{"id":"c","text":"Magnetic reconnection"},{"id":"d","text":"Planetary rotation"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Above it, infalling material can be blown away.","explanation":"The Eddington limit occurs when outward radiation force balances gravitational attraction.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q01","level":1,"category":"Computation","tags":["addition"],"prompt":"What is 9 + 6?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"15"},{"id":"b","text":"17"},{"id":"c","text":"14"},{"id":"d","text":"16"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Combine ten and five.","explanation":"9 + 6 = 15.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q02","level":2,"category":"Computation","tags":["multiplication"],"prompt":"What is 14 × 4?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"48"},{"id":"b","text":"42"},{"id":"c","text":"64"},{"id":"d","text":"56"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Double 14 twice.","explanation":"14 × 4 = 56.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q03","level":3,"category":"Computation","tags":["division"],"prompt":"What is 81 ÷ 9?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"12"},{"id":"b","text":"9"},{"id":"c","text":"8"},{"id":"d","text":"10"}],"correctChoiceId":"b","hint":"Think of a square number.","explanation":"81 ÷ 9 = 9.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q04","level":4,"category":"Computation","tags":["percentages"],"prompt":"What is 25% of 60?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"15"},{"id":"b","text":"10"},{"id":"c","text":"20"},{"id":"d","text":"25"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"One quarter of 60 is required.","explanation":"25% is one quarter, and one quarter of 60 is 15.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q05","level":5,"category":"Computation","tags":["decimals"],"prompt":"What is 2.5 × 8?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"24"},{"id":"b","text":"18"},{"id":"c","text":"16"},{"id":"d","text":"20"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Split 2.5 into 2 and 0.5.","explanation":"2 × 8 plus 0.5 × 8 equals 16 + 4 = 20.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q06","level":6,"category":"Computation","tags":["averages"],"prompt":"What is the average of 9, 15, and 18?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"14"},{"id":"b","text":"15"},{"id":"c","text":"12"},{"id":"d","text":"13"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Their sum is 42.","explanation":"42 divided by 3 is 14.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q07","level":7,"category":"Computation","tags":["powers"],"prompt":"What is 3³ − 5?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"26"},{"id":"b","text":"22"},{"id":"c","text":"18"},{"id":"d","text":"24"}],"correctChoiceId":"b","hint":"Evaluate the cube first.","explanation":"3³ is 27, and 27 − 5 = 22.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q08","level":8,"category":"Computation","tags":["fractions"],"prompt":"What is two-fifths of 75?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"30"},{"id":"b","text":"35"},{"id":"c","text":"45"},{"id":"d","text":"25"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"One fifth of 75 is 15.","explanation":"Two fifths of 75 is 2 × 15 = 30.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q09","level":9,"category":"Computation","tags":["percentages"],"prompt":"Increasing 80 by 25% gives what result?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"120"},{"id":"b","text":"105"},{"id":"c","text":"100"},{"id":"d","text":"90"}],"correctChoiceId":"c","hint":"A quarter of 80 is 20.","explanation":"80 + 20 = 100.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q10","level":10,"category":"Computation","tags":["rates"],"prompt":"A train travels at 60 miles per hour for 2.5 hours. How far does it travel?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"135 miles"},{"id":"b","text":"180 miles"},{"id":"c","text":"150 miles"},{"id":"d","text":"120 miles"}],"correctChoiceId":"c","hint":"Multiply speed by time.","explanation":"60 × 2.5 = 150 miles.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q11","level":11,"category":"Computation","tags":["series"],"prompt":"What is the sum of the first ten positive odd numbers?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"100"},{"id":"b","text":"110"},{"id":"c","text":"90"},{"id":"d","text":"121"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"The sum of the first n odd numbers is n².","explanation":"For n = 10, the sum is 10² = 100.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q12","level":12,"category":"Computation","tags":["combinatorics"],"prompt":"How many ways can two people be chosen from a group of eight?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"28"},{"id":"b","text":"16"},{"id":"c","text":"56"},{"id":"d","text":"32"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Use 8 × 7, then divide by 2.","explanation":"The combination is 8 choose 2 = 28.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q13","level":13,"category":"Computation","tags":["mental math"],"prompt":"What is 125 × 48?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"5750"},{"id":"b","text":"6000"},{"id":"c","text":"6250"},{"id":"d","text":"6500"}],"correctChoiceId":"b","hint":"Use 48 × (100 + 25).","explanation":"4800 + 1200 = 6000.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q14","level":14,"category":"Computation","tags":["fractions"],"prompt":"What is 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"1"},{"id":"b","text":"5/6"},{"id":"c","text":"7/6"},{"id":"d","text":"3/2"}],"correctChoiceId":"a","hint":"Use denominator 6.","explanation":"3/6 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 6/6 = 1.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01-q15","level":15,"category":"Computation","tags":["algebra","identities"],"prompt":"If a + b = 10 and ab = 21, what is a² + b²?","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"79"},{"id":"b","text":"42"},{"id":"c","text":"100"},{"id":"d","text":"58"}],"correctChoiceId":"d","hint":"Use (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b².","explanation":"100 = a² + b² + 42, so a² + b² = 58.","usage":{"freshMix":false,"setIds":["builtin-set-computation-01"]}}],"sets":[{"id":"builtin-set-astronomy-01","title":"Astronomy and Space: First Light","description":"Fifteen astronomy questions progressing from familiar Solar System facts to advanced stellar physics.","theme":"Astronomy and Space","tags":["single-category","space"],"questionIds":["builtin-set-astronomy-01-q01","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q02","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q03","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q04","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q05","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q06","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q07","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q08","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q09","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q10","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q11","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q12","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q13","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q14","builtin-set-astronomy-01-q15"]},{"id":"builtin-set-computation-01","title":"Computation Challenge: Mental Gymnastics","description":"Fifteen mental-computation questions designed to reward clean arithmetic, pattern recogni